A||||22||Wannabe||Safety Warning - Please Read!||||4||
Z||000000||Wannabe||09-10-2001||11:53 PM||||Ok, this may sound kind of strange, but it was one of those too bizarre to be tru stories that actually happened. A friend was working underneath his car when he heard the distinctive sound of steel snapping apart. Next thing he new, one of the four two ton jack stands that was holding up the car collapsed. To make matters worse, the collapse caused the car to shift to the side, tipping the other stands. The only thing that save him was the fact that the jack had been left patrly extened under the car, plus the help of three other guys who were with him. What is so shocking about this story is the idea that a jack stand, something that you learn to trust implicitly to do a very basic job, provided you use it properly, could just fail so completely. My friend escaped with just a couple of cracked ribs, but it could have been much worse. And in case you think Fieros are too light to break a jack stand, the car he was working on was a Cavalier. Yikes. ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Leadfoot||09-11-2001||12:28 AM||||Wow! That was a close call.  I would sue the jackstand manufacturer....or write them a letter detailing the event.  What is the world coming to....you can't even trust your jack stand!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||09-11-2001||12:33 AM||||if you can't trust a jack stand, what can you trust?!?!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spiff||09-11-2001||01:25 AM||||Glad to hear he wasn't hurt. I never trust just the jack stands. I always leave the loor jack under the car and raised just in case something happens. Better safe than sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000004||pontiacs4me||09-11-2001||01:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spiff:<BR><B>Glad to hear he wasn't hurt. I never trust just the jack stands. I always leave the loor jack under the car and raised just in case something happens. Better safe than sorry.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I never use jackstands, I just use the floor jack.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sleeper||09-11-2001||02:00 AM||||i dislike jackstands too but i use them and the floorjack too for safety reasons. my fiero way to low to the ground, it would probably crush me if the stands broke.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Auto<BR>17" Racing Hart C5 wheels, Kumho 712 tires, Intrax springs, KYB shocks, KYB-GR2 struts, West Coast Fiero Strut Tower Bar, Addco Front & Rear Swaybars, West Coast Fiero Cold-Air Intake w/ HKS filter, Beretta brake upgrade in front, KVS Performance Slotted Rotors in rear, Earl's Steel-Braided Brake Lines, custom burnt J.E.T. super chip and MSD Blaster Coil.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Sleeper (edited 09-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||09-11-2001||07:58 AM||||I usually leave the floor jack under mine, as well as the jack stands. Now that I've read this, I'll be <I>especially</I> sure to.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Thanks for sharing.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||14
Z||000007||Denny||09-11-2001||08:06 AM||||Wannabe,<P>You didn't mention if the jack stands were new or old, their condition, or if they were high-quality or el-cheapo.<P>I only buy high-quality jacks and jack stands, and inspect all regularly.<P>BTW, I always wince when I see someone under a car with only a jack for support.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wannabe||09-11-2001||09:05 AM||||The stands were 2 ton rated, not the cheapest 3 legged kind that are made from split tubing, but the sort that "look" sturdy. They were from Canadadian Tire, a popular automotive/discount store. At first, they refused to give a refund for the price of the stands - liability probably rests first and foremost with the store though, as they are the one responsible for buying these things from any one of dozens of far east suppliers - price is king. The kicker is that it probably costs the factories that make these things about $2 for a jack stand. Personally, I use 3-ton, formed and seam welded stands, and the jack stays under the car, somewhere out of the way.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||09-11-2001||03:22 PM||||SAVE the failed stand. Do Not discard it. Even if your friend doesn't sue. Contact the Consumer Product Safety Commision from the link in my cave and file a formal complaint.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Alex4mula||09-11-2001||04:11 PM||||At the beginnings of my Fiero days (back in 88) I was stupid enough to use the Fiero tire jack and a small single post hydraulic jack to lift the car to change the oil and filter. One day I was lucky enough to be on the side when I saw the car jack bending over to the side and see the car almost fall (I held it). Those young days!!<BR>Now I always use two sturdy towers and always leave the floor jack holding just in case.||||reg||1
Z||000011||StuGood||09-11-2001||04:34 PM||||For once I'll keep it short.  Suffice to say there are plenty of jacks and stands in my garage.  And I use them.<P>When you're too tired to go over and grab another stand, you're too tired to be working.      ||||reg||4
Z||000012||Indyellowgt||09-11-2001||06:21 PM||||WHOA....<BR>Man,that has GOT to be the scarriest feeling.<BR>You know what is wierd?? I came home from work tonite to find an 85 GT I am parting out,in the same shape.I had left it supported with 2 stands AND my floor jack.<BR>Somehow it twisted,and crashed hard.While noone was near it(thank god),and the stand itself did not "fail", it just fell over,but all the same,I was underneath that car yesterday.Scary.And to make matters worse it ripped the oil pan open when it fell.Nice mess for me.<BR>Later.<P>-Mark   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000013||roadwarrior||09-11-2001||10:34 PM||||well for those of us that don't have jack stands, I use cinder blocks that are about 1'by 6" by 5" and they hold up a tractor trailer so I trust them, but always leave the jack there too, just in case ||||reg||1
Z||000014||cadaver||09-11-2001||11:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by roadwarrior:<BR><B>well for those of us that don't have jack stands, I use cinder blocks that are about 1'by 6" by 5" and they hold up a tractor trailer so I trust them, but always leave the jack there too, just in case </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>NEVER NEVER NEVER USE A CINDER BLOCK TO SUPPORT A CAR.  You may think the are fine but when they go they suddenly turn to dust. No creak and groan like a stand but explode suddenly. And if you drop one and dont realize its cracked. They crack very easy. Then its all over.<BR>If you guys are afraid of using stands now use big blocks of wood like railroad ties.<BR>But a good quality stand will be fine. Not that cheap crapp they sell at the discount/ripoff joints.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||WKDFIRO||09-12-2001||12:00 AM||||There times when I know Im going to be under a car for a while, like the time I had to drill out a broken stud in starter hole, I put an old tire (still on the rim) next to where I am working under the car especially if I have the wheels off in the air. I figure if it shifts or falls it would land on the wheel first before it crushes me! And it might just give me enough space to scramble out especially if Im in the garage or driveway by myself. <P>I use jack stands and floor jacks all the time but I do it just for peace of mind. thankfully I havnen't had to find out if it works!||||reg||14
Z||000016||NEPTUNE||09-12-2001||12:12 PM||||roger on using the jack or spare as a back up in case the stand fails. It costs nothing to be safe.<BR>If the worst happens, in most states your auto no fault or pip insurance will cover this injury.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Voytek||09-12-2001||12:45 PM||||I'm thinking now that maybe the $12 CDN/pair from SuperStore I got aren't the best jacks... hmmm<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||87GTZ34||09-12-2001||05:12 PM||||I use stands, leave the jack (up) under the car and use 4x4s (wood) piled up.  Paranoid yes! When I was 19, I was putting a transmission in my GFs parents DeVille using a floor jack.  I always work alone as that is the most efficient for me.  Anyway, had problems getting 2 of the bolts started, finally got them all in went to get the wrench and realized I was stuck.  Yes the jack leaked which I knew, but I didn't think I would get stuck... After about 20 minutes of yelling, my brother (the only one home at the time) came out to the garage (it was detached) not because he heard me but it was time for him to go to work....  So this is how I became so paranoid.  Safety is like fun... you can never ever have too much of it.<P>edited for typos<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 09-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||mrfixit58||09-12-2001||05:27 PM||||When I was a young buck, an old mechanic who often did freelance work for our gas station, showed up with a big bandaid across his nose and a broken lens in his glasses. When I asked what happened, he told he the jack he was using to hold up the car he was working under collapsed. Fortunatly, the wheels were on the car so it didn't crush his head but the car bounced down far ehought for a U-bolt for the exhaust pipe hit the left lens of his glasses. The lens cracked and the nose piece gouged his nose pretty good but otherwise, he was OK. He then showed me his brand new jack stands in the back seat of his car.<P>As a safety precaution, I ALWAYS use jackstands and I ALWAY grab the bumper and shake the car BEFORE I get under it. It only takes about 2-3 minutes. <P>Work safely,<P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR> [IMG]http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG [/IMG]  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfpole.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000020||StuGood||09-12-2001||05:32 PM||||Wow-!  Cheap lesson, eh, 87GTZ34 - ?<P>FWIW, I have a cheapie cordless phone set up in my garage.  Have taken the receiver with me under the car on occasion, just for that little extra security.  That way, I wouldn't have to yell  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  So rude to yell  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   ||||reg||11
Z||000021||artherd||09-13-2001||03:31 AM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/northstar/drop-cradle.jpg">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>You cannot have too much support under your car. I never use fewer than FOUR jackstands PER side (two to hold load, two set one notch lower, just so I will never need them   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Total of 8 jackstands here.    I also have an 6-ton ~100lb floor jack just for lifting the car. I don't want that to ever fail.   <P>Biggest thing is I inspect *every* stand prior to use.  If there is *ANY* rust, I throw it out.<P>If a Fiero ever fell on one of us, we would be dead, simple as that. No bouncy clearance   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">   <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000022||87GTZ34||09-13-2001||11:30 AM||||Stu - It was a fortunate lesson and message from above.  Ironically, we had 4 jackstands sitting in the corner of the garage.  I was just to inexperienced to think of the consequences.  At that point, I had already been working on heavy equipment for two years and never witnessed a mishap.  Of course I was 'only' working on a lightweight car in that incident so why waste time & energy with jackstands?  That was the lesson that I took from that experience...  in whatever situation there is/are always consequence(s) its just a question of what are they.||||reg||1
